"1. AIDS. 2026 Sep 1;40(9):1271-1279. doi: 10.1097/QAD.0000000000004540. Epub 2026\n May 7.\n\nRisks and possible implications of weight gain after switch to dolutegravir in a \nlarge Ugandan cohort of people with HIV.\n\nOdongpiny ELA(1)(2), Otaalo B(1), Musaazi J(3), Nalugga EA(3), Kiguba R(4), \nNgabirano D(1), Ddungu A(1), Owarwo N(1), Kesby M(2), Holden M(2), Castelnuovo \nB(3), Semeere A(1), Sloan DJ(2), Sekaggya-Wiltshire C(3).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)Prevention, Care and Treatment Department, Infectious Diseases Institute, \nKampala, Uganda.\n(2)School of Medicine, University of St. Andrews, Fife, St Andrews, Scotland.\n(3)Research Department, Infectious Diseases Institute.\n(4)Department of Pharmacology and Therapeutics, College of Health Sciences, \nMakerere University, Kampala, Uganda.\n\nOBJECTIVES: We investigated weight gain amongst people with HIV (PWH) in Kampala \nafter switching their antiretroviral therapy (ART) anchor drug from nevirapine, \nefavirenz, or a protease inhibitor to dolutegravir.\nDESIGN: This was a retrospective cohort analysis.\nMETHODS: Data were extracted from electronic medical records of nonpregnant PWH \nwho were virally suppressed on dolutegravir-based therapy for at least 12\u200amonths \nfollowing switch. We studied the rate of weight gain using a piecewise spline \nrandom effects model and used Cox-proportional hazard models to describe risk \nfactors for weight gain more than 10% after dolutegravir switch. We assessed the \npotential impact if we applied proposed switch criteria for away from \ndolutegravir, proposed by an expert group including ADVANCE trial investigators.\nRESULTS: We included 5489 PWH, 59.6% of whom were female individuals, who had \nswitched to dolutegravir after a median duration of 9 [interquartile range \n(IQR): 6.1-12.8] years of prior ART. The rate of weight gain was 0.12\u200akg/month \n[95% confidence interval (CI): 0.11 to -0.14] in the first 12\u200amonths postswitch \nbut varied by prior anchor drug. The cumulative risk of at least 10% weight gain \nby 36\u200amonths was 37.8%, and this was more likely if they were female, aged \n25-49\u200ayears or their previous ART included efavirenz or a protease inhibitor but \nless likely if the preswitch BMI was at least 25\u200akg/m 2 . Overall, 15.7% would \nbe eligible for further ART change according to proposed switch criteria.\nCONCLUSION: We found significant weight gain regardless of the pre- dolutegravir \n(DTG) regimen. More than a third of PWH had weight gain of more than 10% by 36\u200a \nmonths.\n\nCopyright \u00a9 2026 Wolters Kluwer Health, LLC. All rights reserved.\n\nDOI: 10.1097/QAD.0000000000004540\nPMID: 42097858 [Indexed for MEDLINE]"